From cd5f007f045169cc48096f6ac1d1af9dc27783e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 00:32:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Measure the property map's real size distribution
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 2-7 is blocked on a measurement, not a design. `--object-alloc`
showed that a property map's memory is a fixed block rather than
per-field storage: VirtualMemory.Allocate rounds its first request up to
16 nodes, so one field costs the same as three and ~1 040 B more than
none. A smaller floor halves a one-field object and makes an eight-field
object worse by paying repeated resize-and-copy. Which side wins is a
question about the distribution of REAL objects, and every synthetic
probe answers it by construction.

PropertyStorageMetrics records, per SAUint32Map<T> value type, the final
node-group count of each map: an allocation moves its map out of the
previous bucket and into the next, so histogram[k] ends up holding the
number of maps whose life ended at k groups. A map that never allocated
is an object with no named properties and is counted nowhere, which is
correct — it never pays the floor. Also counts the backing-array resizes
and the nodes they copy, because that is the cost a smaller floor trades
FOR and it should be measured rather than assumed.

Disabled by default and the recording path is not reached at all while
it is: SAUint32Map tests the flag before reading the two counters the
measurement needs, and holds its histogram in a per-instantiation static
so the path is a field read rather than a dictionary lookup.

--property-map-distribution <octane-dir> runs Octane's own suites, one
fresh context each with the histogram reset between them so a per-suite
disagreement is visible instead of averaged away, and simulates each
candidate growth policy against the result. The simulation mirrors
VirtualMemory.Allocate step for step rather than modelling it, and takes
the node size from SAUint32Map<T>.NodeSizeBytes rather than from a
hand-added field list, so the arithmetic cannot drift from the code it is
about. A suite that throws still reports the maps it built first, and the
failure is named in the output.

The corpus is not vendored, so the emitter takes a checkout path.
BROILER_MAP_DISTRIBUTION_RUNS sets runs per benchmark (default 10) and
BROILER_MAP_DISTRIBUTION_SKIP sets suites to leave out — the first so the
claim that the distribution converges can be checked rather than assumed,
the second because everything runs in one process with no per-suite
timeout and Mandreel needs minutes just to compile.

First result confirms the model the item was built on: a node is 56 bytes,
so the 16-node floor is 16 x 56 + 24 = 920 B for any object carrying a
named property, and one field and three fields both land inside a single
block.

Benchmarks and storage instrumentation only; no change to engine
behaviour. Suite: 7 392 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.

Refs docs/performance-roadmap.md item 2-7.
